          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ਦਿਨਚਰਿਆ (dinchariyaa) — dinchariā — daily routine; the usual ordered activities of a day."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hear and say the whole word before reading it. Keep it as one whole noun rather than inventing smaller Punjabi pieces. This lesson owns the word alone; the complete identity frame comes only after a separate retrieval."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "cognates",
          "title": "Across the family",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "An Australian Government Punjabi health page uses this exact word in an everyday routine context. A Punjabi-supported A2 lesson also uses it in its daily-routine heading. Together they support only the present-day daily-routine sense taught here; this lesson makes no claim about other specialised uses. Sources: Australian Government Medicare Mental Health Check In; MozhiLingo: Daily Routine."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "For source history, this learned Punjabi word is from Sanskrit dinacaryā. Apte records that Sanskrit form with the sense “daily occupation, daily routine of business.” That history does not turn its parts into a productive Punjabi rule: keep the present-day Punjabi noun whole. Source: V. S. Apte, The Practical Sanskrit-English Dictionary, p. 0813-1, Cologne Digital Sanskrit Dictionaries."
            }
          ]
        },
